Question
The empire brought few economic opportunities to Britain in the year 1890-1914

Arguments for:-

- Parts of the empire had large amounts of raw materials that were highly valuable to Britain
- India was 20% (50 million) of Britain's total exports by 1914
- Between 1900 and 1914 Canada's area for growing wheat for Britain increased sixfold
- People still wanted the Empire economically - e.g. voting liberals in 1906
- 1914 India has 0% Tariff on British goods and 40% on goods from the US
- What happened to British overseas investment: Doubled between 1900 - 1913 from £2billion to £4bn

- India was still a rock for Britain and other colonies provided raw materials or food

Arguments against:-

- More trade with Belgium in this period than with Sub-Saharan Africa
- Imperial federation league disbanded 1893
- 1897 12% of trade came from tropical Africa
- In 1913, what was the divide of empire to non empire for imports: 25% to 75%
- In 1913, what was the divide between empire to non empire exports: 37% to 63%
- 1896 Trade = 745 million pounds
	- empire only 183 million pounds
- Although India was self financing and the white colonies only relied on Britain for defence, others had to be subsidised and the cost of acquiring some of the Empire far outweighed any returns on it. The cost of the Navy was also huge.
- What was Britain's share of world trade: 1880:23%, 1910:17%
- What was Britain's share of world trade by 1914: Britain: 15% Germany: 16% USA: 35%

# Judgement

**************************************************************************************************

To what extent did Britain's economic relationship with its Empire change during the years 1914 to 1939?

For:-

- 1931 Britain abandoned gold standard (+ Sterling Area) and empire provided stability by fixing their currencies to the sterling
	- Britain introduced a general 10% tax on imports, but the crown colonies were exempt; Britain and the dominions gave each other's exports, preferential treatment in their own markets
- Britain started to depend more on its colonies
- 1932 Ottawa conference - imperial preference
- Britain raised taxes on colonies
	- Placed higher taxes on Indian imports to generate revenue to bolster their control during the rise of the nationalist movement, from 11% in 1917 to 25% in 1931.
- Overall change in financial sectors:
	- as economic burden of the empire grew, their ability to pay for it diminished. This problem intensified during the great depression, which saw a collapse of international trade.

Against:-

- India still remained the most important colony and was heavily important to trade
	- 2/3 of Indian imports were British in 1914

Judgement:-

It changed majorly due to Britain's shifting economic position in the world. Britain had less economic power and especially the great depression pushed Britain's exports down and this majorly shifted the relationship it had with its empire.

